# carbocation rearrangement

1. CORE THEORY AND DEFINITIONS

Haloalkanes (Alkyl Halides) are chemical compounds formed by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms in an aliphatic hydrocarbon with halogen atoms (Fluorine, Chlorine, Bromine, or Iodine). The halogen atom is attached to an sp3 hybridized carbon atom.

Haloarenes (Aryl Halides) are formed by replacing hydrogen atoms in an aromatic hydrocarbon with halogen atoms. In these compounds, the halogen is directly attached to an sp2 hybridized carbon atom of the aromatic ring.

The general representation of these compounds is R-X for haloalkanes and Ar-X for haloarenes, where X represents the halogen.

2. NOMENCLATURE SYSTEMS

There are two primary systems for naming these compounds: the Common System and the IUPAC System.

Common Names:
Haloalkanes are named as Alkyl halides. This involves naming the alkyl group (methyl, ethyl, propyl, etc.) followed by the halide (fluoride, chloride, bromide, iodide). For haloarenes, the common name is Aryl halide.

IUPAC Names:
Haloalkanes are named as Haloalkanes. The halogen is treated as a substituent (fluoro, chloro, bromo, iodo) followed by the name of the parent alkane chain. For haloarenes, the IUPAC name is Haloarene.
    Ex : CH3CH2Cl
        Common name - Ethyl chloride
        IUPAC name - Chloroethane

3. CLASSIFICATION

i) On the Basis of Number of Halogen Atoms
Compounds are classified based on how many halogen atoms are present in the molecule:

Mono-haloalkanes/Mono-haloarenes: Contain only one halogen atom.
Example: CH3CH2X (Ethyl halide/Haloethane).

Di-haloalkanes/Di-haloarenes: Contain two halogen atoms.
Example: CH2X2 (Methylene halide/Dihalomethane).

    NOMENCLATURE OF DIHALIDES
        Dihalides have specific naming conventions in the common system based on the relative positions of the two halogen atoms:

    Geminal Dihalides (Gem-dihalides):
        Both halogen atoms are attached to the same carbon atom. In the common system, these are named as "Alkylidene halides".
    Example: CH3CHCl2 is named Ethylidene chloride.

    Vicinal Dihalides (Vic-dihalides):
        Halogen atoms are attached to two adjacent carbon atoms. In the common system, these are named as "Alkylene dihalides".
    Example: CH2BrCH2Br is named Ethylene dibromide.

Poly-haloalkanes: Contain three or more halogen atoms.
Example: CHCl3 (Chloroform/Trichloromethane) and CCl4 (Carbon tetrachloride/Tetrachloromethane).

ii) Compounds Containing sp3 C-X Bond
a) Alkyl Halides (sp3 C-X):
Classified as Primary (1 degree), Secondary (2 degree), or Tertiary (3 degree) depending on the degree of the carbon atom to which the halogen is attached.
1ᵒ : Halogen on a carbon attached to one other carbon.
2ᵒ : Halogen on a carbon attached to two other carbons.
3ᵒ : Halogen on a carbon attached to three other carbons.

b) Allylic Halides (sp3 C-X):
The halogen is attached to an sp3 hybridized carbon atom which is adjacent to a carbon-carbon double bond (C=C). This carbon is called the allylic carbon.
@ photo - halo1
Benzylic Halides (sp3 C-X): The halogen is attached to an sp3 hybridized carbon atom which is adjacent to an aromatic (benzene) ring.

iii) Compounds Containing sp2 C-X Bond
a) Vinylic Halides (sp2 C-X):
The halogen is attached directly to one of the sp2 hybridized carbon atoms of a carbon-carbon double bond (C=C).    # The sp2 hybridised atom can't be part of an aromatic ring.

b) Aryl Halides (sp2 C-X):
The halogen is attached directly to an sp2 hybridized carbon atom of an aromatic ring.

