7. CONCEPT CONNECTIONS

-Nomenclature of haloalkanes and haloarenes is built upon the basic rules of IUPAC nomenclature learned in Class 11 Hydrocarbons.
-Understanding the sp3 vs sp2 hybridization of the carbon bearing the halogen is critical for predicting reactivity in later sections of the chapter (SN1/SN2 vs ESR/NSR).
- Hybridization Impact: Understanding sp3 vs sp2 attachment is vital because sp2 C-X bonds (Aryl and Vinyl halides) are shorter, stronger, and show partial double bond character due to resonance, making them less reactive toward Nucleophilic Substitution Reactions (NSR).
- Degree and Stability: The degree of the halide (1, 2, or 3) directly dictates the stability of the carbocation formed during SN1 reactions (3 degree is most stable).
- Benzylic/Allylic Reactivity: Although attached to sp3 carbons, allylic and benzylic halides are highly reactive toward both SN1 and SN2 because their transition states or carbocations are stabilized by resonance with the adjacent pi system.

